schemaVersion: "2"
kind: mixin
name: yoki-codex
requires:
  agent: codex
displayName: yoki base tools for Codex
description: >-
  Fills the same base-image gaps the Claude kit fills, so a sandboxed Codex
  session has the same shell and toolchain manager as a sandboxed Claude one.

# Deliberately NOT a .template: unlike yoki-claude this kit embeds no host
# path, because nothing in claude-profiles/ applies to Codex. yoki-box keys the
# read-only dotfiles mounts off the presence of a spec.yaml.template, so a kit
# written as a plain spec.yaml gets no mounts — which is correct here.
#
# Measured on the Codex base image 2026-08-15 (Ubuntu, user `agent`, home
# /home/agent, codex-cli 0.146.0): node, git, jq and rg are PRESENT; zsh, mise
# and fd are MISSING — the same three gaps as the Claude image, so the install
# list is identical.
#
# What this kit does NOT do, and why:
#
# 1. It does not write ~/.codex/config.toml. sbx already writes one at create
#    time ("yolo mode": approval_policy = "never", sandbox_mode =
#    "danger-full-access") and that same file carries the mcp-gateway server
#    with its `Bearer proxy-managed` header. Overwriting it would silently cut
#    MCP off. CODEX_HOME is set to /home/agent/.codex.
#
# 2. It therefore leaves Codex more permissive than Claude in the same posture.
#    Claude runs `auto` — a classifier reviews every action — because a microVM
#    stops destruction but not disclosure (/run/sandbox/source is readable and
#    the Balanced policy allows 192 domains). Codex has no config-level
#    equivalent of `auto`: `on-request` leaves the decision to the model, which
#    a prompt injection simply declines to use, and `untrusted` asks about
#    everything. The nearest real equivalent is the `--approve-for-me` launch
#    flag (automatic review). Left unwired because sbx's own launch arguments
#    for the codex agent are not introspectable, so overriding them here would
#    be guesswork. Pass it yourself when it matters:
#        ycodex -- --approve-for-me
#    or layer a profile: put yoki.config.toml under $CODEX_HOME and run
#        ycodex -- -p yoki

permissions:
  network:
    allow:
      # apt on arm64 Ubuntu resolves to ports.ubuntu.com, not archive.
      - ports.ubuntu.com
      - archive.ubuntu.com
      - security.ubuntu.com
      # `curl https://mise.run | sh` redirects mise.run -> mise.jdx.dev, and
      # the installer pulls the binary from the GitHub release assets.
      - mise.run
      - mise.jdx.dev
      - github.com
      - objects.githubusercontent.com
      - release-assets.githubusercontent.com

setup:
  install:
    - command: apt-get update && apt-get install -y zsh fd-find
      description: The only base-image gaps besides mise (fd ships as fd-find on Ubuntu)
    - command: curl -fsSL https://mise.run | sh
      user: "1000"
      description: Install mise as the agent user so toolchains land in /home/agent
